  <h2>Why a Romanian SIM is worth it (and when it isn't)</h2>
  <p>
    A local number makes daily life in Cluj smoother: SMS bus tickets to 7479
    (<a href="/cluj/guides/public-transport-cluj/">which don't work on foreign SIMs</a>), delivery
    apps and couriers who call, appointment confirmations, and often the phone number a bank or
    landlord expects. Mobile data in Romania is famously cheap and fast.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The exception:</strong> if you're staying only a few weeks and you have an EU SIM,
    EU roam-like-at-home rules may cover you comfortably — check your home plan's fair-use data
    cap before buying anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Prepaid vs contract</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Prepaid ("cartelă")</strong> is the newcomer default: buy a starter pack, activate a
    monthly bundle, top up as you go. No commitment, no Romanian paperwork history required, and
    if a plan doesn't suit you, you've lost very little.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Contracts (subscriptions)</strong> are usually a bit better value per gigabyte but
    tie you in — typically 12–24 months — and the sign-up may involve more documentation and a
    Romanian address. Sensible once you know you're staying; unnecessary in week one.
  </p>

  <h2>What ID you need</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your <strong>passport</strong> (or EU ID card). Registration requirements for prepaid
    SIMs in Romania have shifted over the years and store practices vary — some sales points
    ask for ID and register the SIM, others sell starter packs off the shelf. Rather than
    memorizing the current rule, just have your ID with you; it's never wrong, and for contracts
    it's always required (often alongside proof of address or residence documents).
  </p>

  <h2>Where to buy in Cluj</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Operator shops</strong> — the reliable option, with staff who can activate a
        bundle for you and usually manage some English. All major operators have stores in the
        city centre and in the malls (Iulius Mall and VIVO!).</li>
    <li><strong>Kiosks and supermarkets</strong> — starter packs are often sold at press kiosks
        and larger supermarkets; fine if you're comfortable activating a bundle yourself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The main operators are <a href="https://www.digi.ro" rel="noopener">Digi</a>,
    <a href="https://www.orange.ro" rel="noopener">Orange</a>,
    <a href="https://www.vodafone.ro" rel="noopener">Vodafone</a> and
    <a href="https://www.telekom.ro" rel="noopener">Telekom</a>. All four cover Cluj city well;
    real differences show up in rural coverage and price structure. We won't crown a "best"
    one — current offers change too often for that to be honest. Compare the operators' prepaid
    pages on the day you buy.
  </p>

  <h2>eSIM</h2>
  <p>
    Major Romanian operators offer eSIM on many plans, but availability differs by operator,
    plan type and even sales channel — confirm on the operator's own eSIM page (or in the shop)
    before you count on it, especially for prepaid. If your phone is dual-SIM, the pragmatic
    newcomer setup is: home SIM stays in for banking codes from home, Romanian SIM handles
    everything local.
  </p>

  <h2>How not to overpay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Say what you actually need: "prepaid, mostly data, one month" — and decline add-ons.</li>
    <li>Don't buy at the airport if you can wait an hour; city shops have the full range.</li>
    <li>Check that the bundle price you're quoted is the <em>recurring</em> price, not a promo
        that doubles next month.</li>
    <li>Short stay? A one-month prepaid bundle beats any contract, whatever the salesperson's
        commission says.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Standing in a phone shop, unsure whether the plan you're being offered makes sense? Message
    us the details — <a href="/cluj/foreigner-support/">a local sanity check</a> takes minutes
    and has saved people from a year of paying for television channels they didn't know they'd
    ordered.
  </p>
